WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.307
- What is your favorite organ
to handle in the human body?

00:00:03.307 --> 00:00:05.430
- Oh, the brain, obviously.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.263
- Really?

00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:08.470
What happens when you go six feet under?

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.690
Well, I'm talking about that today,

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.440
while watching "Six Feet Under,"

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.770
with the special guest, Caitlin Doughty.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.672
Let's do it. Ask a mortician.

00:00:16.672 --> 00:00:17.540
Be-woo!

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.770
Tell me how you found yourself

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.210
in this very unique career position.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.511
What drew you to this field?

00:00:23.511 --> 00:00:26.300
- (laughs) I was always
interested in death.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.190
I went to college at the
University of Chicago.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:29.620
I was a medieval history major.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.470
There was a lot of death
in the Middle Ages,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.870
and that was kind of my focus.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.640
But I wanted to see what
happened in real 20th century,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.700
21st-century American death.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.760
Got a job at a crematory,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.150
and I've been doing this for 13 years now.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.130
And have never left.
- Wow.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.510
Oftentimes, the subject of
death is a very taboo subject.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.940
How did you decide to start
a YouTube channel on it

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.780
and also approach it with a respectful

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.140
yet lighthearted manner?

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.060
Which I do with medicine all the time.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:55.893
It's very different

00:00:55.893 --> 00:00:57.220
when you're talking about strictly death.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.430
How did you find yourself here?

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.890
- Well, I think it's
probably some of the same

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.300
struggles that you have,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.100
which is knowing that a spoonful of sugar

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.250
helps the medicine go down.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.670
You want to create a cone of safety

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:09.700
around what you're doing,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.867
so when they're watching
your channel, people go,

00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:16.017
"Oh, wait a second. It's not
weird to have these questions.

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:18.677
"It's not wrong to have
these questions, or morbid,

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:21.430
"or hypochondriac to
have these questions."

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.858
- Here we go. Our first scene.

00:01:23.858 --> 00:01:26.525
(tray scraping)

00:01:27.447 --> 00:01:29.947
(zip rasping)

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:34.773
- Well?

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.600
- Pretty good corpse, I have to say.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.190
I have a lot of opinions about bad corpses

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.270
in television shows.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.050
This one I would say the blood,

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.540
the kind of sallow skin, the
sinking of the skin already,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.990
I think it's looking pretty...

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.756
I don't know how many corpses you see?

00:01:50.756 --> 00:01:52.330
- (laughs) That's so
funny that you asked that

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.880
because I feel like I work on

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.750
preventing people from becoming corpses.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.170
And then, unfortunately,
once they become corpses,

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.860
I don't have much dealings with them.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.350
So I have less experience.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.440
And I'm gonna look for you to guide me

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.857
on what would be a realistic corpse.

00:02:07.857 --> 00:02:10.420
- Oh, I am so here. I am here for you.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.330
- [Announcer] Top morticians rely

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.160
on Living Splendor embalming fluid.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.910
Living Splendor.
- Wow.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.915
- [Announcer] Only real life is better.

00:02:18.915 --> 00:02:23.070
- (laughs) Is this an
infomercial within the show?

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.710
- It's an infomercial within the show.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.560
I think these infomercials within the show

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.470
are trying to explain the
modern funeral industry,

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.010
a little bit, and this
actually does a fantastic job.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.210
There are embalming fluids
and humectant fluids

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.340
called things like Living Splendor.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.350
The way that they could sell embalming

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.627
and preservation to a
family was by saying,

00:02:42.627 --> 00:02:44.157
"It's not only gonna preserve them,

00:02:44.157 --> 00:02:45.347
"it's gonna keep you safe,

00:02:45.347 --> 00:02:47.860
"and it's gonna offer
a lifelike appearance."

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.340
Which I happen to not agree with.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.870
I think a dead body, you should
look dead because, you know,

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.200
spoiler, that's what they are.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.220
If you don't do anything to the body,

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.270
the body has eyes up, sort of like a....

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.399
Would be my demonstration of that. Like...

00:03:01.399 --> 00:03:03.982
(tense music)

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:06.373
That's a trocar.

00:03:06.373 --> 00:03:07.950
Do you know what that does?
- No.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.360
- In the embalming
where you need to remove

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.300
all of the cavity and organ fluid,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.740
you stab it in, down by the belly button,

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.420
and it has a sucking function.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.070
So you're stabbing and it's sucking,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.090
and out your fluid comes.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.750
And then it's replaced with a
strong preservative chemical.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.290
- What is the strangest
thing you've ever removed

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.560
from the inside of a patient's body?

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.260
- I can't think immediately of something

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.750
that I've pulled out from
inside a body that was strange.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.450
It's more after a cremation.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.790
I would say that something that
even that's sort of normal,

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.240
but it still gets me every
time is titanium hips.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.990
After they come out of
a body, and a cremation,

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:50.160
they have this like beautiful
bluish-green patina.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.857
They almost look like
glamorous sockeye glasses.

00:03:53.857 --> 00:03:55.910
- What happens to the titanium hip?

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.130
- You can ask for it back.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.210
That rarely happens,

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.390
but I love it when a
family asks for it back.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.097
I'm like, "Yes, you've
come to the right place.

00:04:02.097 --> 00:04:03.020
"We will do that for you."

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.330
Oftentimes, it's recycled.
It goes to recycling.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.440
Sometimes it's made into airplane parts.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.603
It's made into street signs.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:12.743
- [Rico] Nate!

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:15.760
What's up!
- This is Rico.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.200
Okay. Rico is fantastic.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.090
Rico went to the same
mortuary college that I did.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:21.410
- Oh, wow.
- He's they're young embalmer

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.160
and he's very enthusiastic

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.390
about being a restorative specialist.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.460
There are certain embalmers

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.140
that have a real deep appreciation

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.010
for what are called hard cases.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.870
Someone's been shot in the head.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.810
Someone hasn't been found for five days.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:42.357
And their job is through wax
and through little gizmos

00:04:42.357 --> 00:04:45.694
and gobits and potions and creams.

00:04:45.694 --> 00:04:48.210
And not really make them
look like themselves,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.620
but can kind of put Humpty
Dumpty back together again

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.360
and bring them into some
resemblance of themselves

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:54.750
so the family can see them.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:55.583
- Ready?

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.343
One, two, three.

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:02.579
(both groaning)
(body thuds)

00:05:02.579 --> 00:05:03.412
- Whoa.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.950
- Angel lust.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:08.940
- Does that happened a lot?

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:10.860
- Yeah. Fair amount. (chuckles)

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.890
You can be dead, but
you're never really dead.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:14.850
- No.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.257
No, no, no, no, no, no.
- (laughs) You're like, "No!

00:05:16.257 --> 00:05:18.330
"You can be really dead."
- Yeah.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:22.530
The idea of angel lust, which
has a post-mortem erection,

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:26.230
is very specific to extremely
rare traumatic deaths.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:29.380
Like, you're suddenly shot in the head,

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:30.710
or you're suddenly strangled,

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.820
or you suddenly have some
sort of traumatic death.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.770
But I personally have never
seen it in an older man

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:38.860
at a nursing home.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:42.300
- So basically, it's like decapitation

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.940
in the midst of intercourse.
- (laughs) Right.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.970
Or maybe like autoerotic asphyxiation.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:48.950
- What's that's smell?

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:52.520
It's like a...
- Disinfectant, burnt coffee.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:53.970
Embalming fluid.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.120
- Yum.
- The smell that I

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.490
always remember from when I was

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.890
in my anatomy cadaver
lab was formaldehyde.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.570
Is that something you guys use a lot?

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:01.403
- It is.

00:06:01.403 --> 00:06:03.550
But actually, the formulant that they use

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:07.920
in anatomy cadaver labs is
actually even more intense

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.050
than what we use in an
average funeral home.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:11.130
That really-

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.620
- Potent.
- Has to have a high index.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.310
- I really appreciated being
able to study the human bodies.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.700
That's what got me into science.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.810
But what I will say was not the easiest,

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.410
was Friday before a Monday exam

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.340
I would be in the cadaver
lab by myself at midnight.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:30.840
Nighttime, sounds of
happening, things moving,

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.670
maybe my imagination playing tricks.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:33.940
Scary, scary stuff,

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.640
but I managed to get through
it and do well on the test.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.060
- That was probably the first time

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.500
you had any meaningful
interaction with a dead body.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.680
I mean, this is kind of
the American condition,

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.300
in some ways, is that
we go our whole lives

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.310
until all of a sudden our mom dies.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.020
Most of us, if you're not
a mortician or doctor,

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.520
all of a sudden you're
faced with this corpse

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:55.980
and you have to figure out
what that means to you.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.660
In an existential way. Is it spiritual?

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.160
Is it a ghost? What is it?

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.670
And you're having all these
reckonings with what it is

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.180
at the worst time of your life.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:05.560
- You know, it's interesting

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:07.720
because I, unfortunately, did lose my mom

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:09.260
right in the middle of med school,

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:10.960
when I was in the cadaver labs.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.170
I will say it's a very
different experience

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:17.070
looking at a body of
someone who you don't know

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.930
versus someone who is close to you.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.840
You expect to feel certain things

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.150
when you're looking at the
body of a family member

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:26.090
that may not come because
it looks different.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.930
Like, you know the person
based on their facial features,

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:32.020
their structure, their little intricacies

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.280
of how they react to the way you speak.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:37.900
And when that's not there,
it almost feels unfamiliar.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.880
Have you ever seen that
happen with family members

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.530
or perhaps, yourself?
- Absolutely.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.460
That to me is actually
the most important reason

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.850
to be with the body.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.427
It can actually be healthy
to look at the body and go,

00:07:48.427 --> 00:07:50.887
"That's not Mom anymore.
That's a shell of her.

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:52.907
"And I see that she's
not suffering anymore

00:07:52.907 --> 00:07:55.050
"'cause she's not all
grimaced up in pain,"

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.930
and that can be comforting
for some people.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.457
And then for other people, that may say,

00:07:58.457 --> 00:08:00.477
"Wait, she looks exactly
like she did in life.

00:08:00.477 --> 00:08:01.747
"She just looks like she's sleeping

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:03.050
"and that's comforting to me."

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.990
I sort of encourage
people to just sit there

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.810
and let what happens happen,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.560
and not put too much pressure on yourself

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:12.030
to feel a certain way when
you're with a dead body

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.290
because you're grieving.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:14.400
And what you really want

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.397
is just to make sure that
the only way out is through.

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:22.427
- "For the velvety look and
feel of actual living tissue."

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.840
No way. (laughs)

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.200
- You do not even wanna know.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.760
They have these plastic,
like, screws, only bigger,

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.850
that they, like, stick
into people's orifices

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:35.300
so stuff won't spill out.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.046
- Tell me about the screws.
(Caitlin laughing)

00:08:37.046 --> 00:08:38.087
- I was like, "Are you
gonna let me get away

00:08:38.087 --> 00:08:38.920
"without responding to that?"

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.490
They're called A-V plugs,

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:45.190
and the A and the V
stand for anal-vaginal.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:47.650
They do kind of look like butt plugs.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:50.350
A plastic kind of swirly butt plug.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.030
After a embalming,

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:54.000
because there's such pressure in the body,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.280
there's always a chance

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:58.030
that the body will purge a
little bit or leak a little bit

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.230
out of whatever open orifices there are.

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:04.830
So you basically get
something like a butt plug

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:05.990
after you die.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:09.340
- What is your favorite organ
to handle in the human body?

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:11.370
- Oh, the brain, obviously.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:12.800
- Really? Okay.
- Yeah.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:13.940
- Why the brain?
- Because it feels like

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.720
you are holding the seat of the soul.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:20.050
Because you're really like
holding who the person was

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:22.740
and their personality and
the seat of their intellect.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.900
- Wow.
- Even if it's just

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:25.440
while you're transferring it

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.290
to sow up the head properly
or whatever you're doing.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:30.970
It just feels like a real privilege to me.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.430
- Hepatitis', syphilis.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.143
Ooh, positive for gonorrhea.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:36.903
- What?

00:09:36.903 --> 00:09:38.520
- Have you ever gone, "Ooh,"

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.030
before giving someone a test result?

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:42.537
Have you ever gone, "Good, good, good!

00:09:42.537 --> 00:09:43.890
"Ooh."
- Ooh.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.840
I don't and for this one reason.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.200
I don't let anything
surprise me in the room.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.710
I review things before
I speak to the patient,

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.740
or I review them before
I called the patient.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.770
'Cause a lot of results
are given by phone.

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:56.170
If I'm gonna be giving a result

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:58.330
that's life-changing in a way,

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.250
I will usually ask the patient
to come in to discuss it.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.660
- How do you psych yourself
up to make that call?

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:05.720
If I knew I had to deliver potentially

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:07.700
life-changing difficult medical news,

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.640
I would just put that off
till the end of the day.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.100
- I don't like to put it
off to the end of the day

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:14.320
'cause it becomes harder.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:18.400
I like to do it as quickly as
possible, meeting as early on,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.160
and as forthcoming.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.850
The more you sort of
bounce around the point,

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.480
the more it causes tension
between you and the patient.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.660
So I try to be a straight
shooter, if you will.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.500
Layout all the facts, get
them to ask questions,

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.900
say the news back to me so they understand

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:36.030
exactly what's going on,
then offer just comfort.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:38.290
So sometimes that could be silence.

